{
  "id": "ss.enemy.charger",
  "name": "Bow and barb",
  "description": "Two animals that are useless apart. The bow-caste is a crossbow laid flat: a stock running the way it travels, head at the leading end, and two prod-limbs thrown out sideways from the shoulders with a tendon strung tip to tip behind them. It has nothing to hurt anything with. The barb-caste lies in the groove down the middle of that stock — a hard point on an abdomen full of something that goes off, and no way of getting anywhere. One winds, the other lands, and both are spent in the same second, which is why neither is drawn to survive it and why the hive only ever sends them in volleys.\n\nIt was a longbow first, stood on end with the barb strung across its middle. That read as a frame and not as an animal facing anywhere, which is the one thing it cannot afford: a longbow is the same shape from either end, and this field is drawn top-down where a body needs a nose. A crossbow has a front. Laid flat it is a body plan an insect can carry without being asked to — long thorax, head at the leading end, limbs out from the shoulders — and the prod sits forward on the stock so most of the animal is behind the thing that throws, which reads as travelling rather than as a bow that happens to be lying down.\n\nTwo lit scent organs on one sprite is the tell that this is two creatures and not one with a decoration, and it costs nothing to say it: the shared organ is already what every body in this roster carries. The barb's rides the neck ahead of its core, because a thing with a point has a front and the lit end belongs at it; the bow's rides its own head.\n\nShapes this could fall back into, all of them tried. Prod-limbs curled to a brace height of 0.45 of the tip-to-tip span came out a horseshoe, then a snail. A sac cut off a shaft at the waist put two ink outlines across each other and the seam read as a join in the middle of an animal, so the point is laid under a closed sac instead, rear buried, and the sac swallows it as it fills.\n\nThe windup is the point. The barb hauls back down the groove while both prod-limbs roll under the load and its core swells — which is the yellow flash the game used to paint over this sprite, drawn instead. On release the bow unwinds through rest and past it while the barb keeps going, because it has left the string. Nothing here points down the line it is about to take: the sprite only ever flips and never rotates, so an arrowhead would promise a heading the engine cannot keep. Drawn facing +x. Same id and radius 10.",
  "tags": ["ss-enemy", "duo"],
  "size": [40, 32],
  "meta": {"radius": 10},
